$DIR/main.p:7:2: cannot store &x in n.next, which outlives x
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of link (declared at main.p:6).
    When link returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but n.next is reached through n,
    which points to memory that link's caller owns and that lives longer. It
    would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value instead of a pointer to it, changing the type of n.next to
    match. This copies x, or moves it if it is an owner:
        n.next = x
    Or allocate x and store its owner, an owned[Node], which moves into n.next:
        n.next = new(Node)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
